{"id":4,"title":"Your Pelvic Floor Deserves the Same Attention as Your Blood Pressure","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Laughing, coughing, or sneezing—and suddenly leaking urine. A persistent urge to rush to the bathroom. A nagging sensation of fullness in your lower abdomen that has no clear explanation. Many women dismiss these signs as normal, or simply part of getting older or having children. But these symptoms are often rooted in an overlooked aspect of health: the pelvic floor.</span></p>\n\n<p>The underlying causes are typically physiological—aging, childbirth, menopause, and hormonal shifts. Yet because pelvic health is rarely discussed or screened, women often delay seeking help. The good news? Early detection can prevent or treat many of these issues. It is time for pelvic floor screening to become as routine as checking your blood pressure.</p>\n\n<h2>What Is the Pelvic Floor?</h2>\n\n<p>The pelvic floor is a group of muscles and tissues that form a supportive hammock at the base of your pelvis. It holds the bladder, uterus, and rectum in their proper positions. When these muscles are weak, overly tight, or uncoordinated, pelvic floor dysfunction can occur. This term describes any condition where the pelvic floor fails to perform its supportive role effectively.</p>\n\n<h2>Recognizing the Symptoms of Pelvic Floor Dysfunction</h2>\n\n<p>Many women attribute pelvic floor symptoms to aging or childbirth, but these signs should never be ignored. Common indicators include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Urinary incontinence</b> – leaking urine when you cough, laugh, sneeze, or exercise</li>\n  <li><b>Frequent urination</b> – needing to go more often than usual, or suddenly</li>\n  <li><b>Bowel control issues</b> – difficulty controlling gas or stool</li>\n  <li><b>Pelvic organ prolapse</b> – a sensation of pressure or a bulge in the vagina, as organs shift downward</li>\n  <li><b>Painful intercourse</b> – discomfort or pain during sex</li>\n  <li><b>Chronic pelvic pain</b> – persistent discomfort in the lower abdomen or pelvic region</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What Increases Your Risk?</h2>\n\n<p>Pregnancy, vaginal childbirth, and menopause are well-known risk factors, but they are not the only ones. Other contributors include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Being overweight or obese</li>\n  <li>Chronic coughing (from smoking, asthma, or allergies)</li>\n  <li>Frequent heavy lifting</li>\n  <li>A sedentary lifestyle</li>\n  <li>High-impact or repetitive exercise without proper core conditioning</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Importantly, women who have never given birth can also experience pelvic floor dysfunction. These issues do not discriminate by age or life stage.</p>\n\n<h2>Is Pelvic Floor Dysfunction Permanent?</h2>\n\n<p>Not necessarily. Many conditions improve significantly with early and appropriate care. Screening is typically simple and painless. It involves discussing your symptoms with a healthcare provider and undergoing a physical examination. In some cases, imaging may be recommended. The earlier a problem is identified, the more options you have for effective treatment.</p>\n\n<p>Incorporating pelvic floor screening into your routine health check-up can reveal muscle weakness long before symptoms like incontinence or prolapse appear. Being proactive—for example, learning how to perform pelvic floor exercises correctly—can help you prevent these issues from developing or worsening.</p>\n\n<h2>What Treatment Options Are Available?</h2>\n\n<p>The vast majority of pelvic floor conditions are treatable. Approaches vary based on the specific issue and its severity, and may include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Pelvic floor physical therapy</b> – guided exercises to strengthen or relax the muscles as needed</li>\n  <li><b>Kegel exercises</b> – when performed with correct technique and consistency, these can improve muscle tone and control</li>\n  <li><b>Lifestyle modifications</b> – such as weight management, proper lifting techniques, and avoiding chronic strain</li>\n  <li><b>Supportive aids</b> – devices like pessaries can help manage prolapse without surgery</li>\n  <li><b>Surgical interventions</b> – in more advanced cases, procedures can restore structural support</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Many women feel embarrassed or uncertain about discussing pelvic health, but these conditions are common, treatable, and far from your fault. Ignoring them can have lasting effects on your quality of life, confidence, and well-being.</p>\n\n<h2>A Call to Prioritize Pelvic Health</h2>\n\n<p>Your pelvic floor works every day—supporting your organs, enabling control, and contributing to comfort. It deserves the same attention as your heart, lungs, or blood pressure. By bringing pelvic health into routine conversations and check-ups, we can shift from silent suffering to proactive care. If you recognize any of the symptoms above, speak with a healthcare professional. Early action makes a difference, and you are not alone.<br><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"/articles/article-images/1779410725_pexels-paulina-vargas-490121449-36833344.jpg","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/P-H-V/FEVE-images/main/article-images/1790035328346_Your-Pelvic-Floor.png","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":true}
{"title":"Comparing the Health Benefits of Watermelon and Muskmelon","img":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/P-H-V/FEVE-images/main/article-images/1790035650390_Watermelon-and-Muskmelon.png","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">As the summer season approaches, our bodies naturally seek ways to stay cool. Eating seasonal foods can help regulate body temperature and is an important practice that has been followed for generations. During hot summer months, two fruits are ideal for staying cool: watermelon and muskmelon. Known for their high water content, they help lower body temperature in extreme heat.</span></p>\n<p>As health and nutrition become more important, a common question is whether one fruit is healthier than the other. Both watermelon and muskmelon offer unique benefits, making them excellent additions to a healthy diet.</p>\n\n<h2>Heart Health and Blood Pressure</h2>\n<p>When it comes to heart health, both melons offer benefits, but muskmelon has a slight edge due to its high potassium content. Potassium is an essential electrolyte that regulates blood pressure and is strongly associated with lower blood pressure and a reduced risk of stroke.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Muskmelon is an excellent source of potassium, making it an ideal choice for managing blood pressure.</li>\n  <li>Watermelon, on the other hand, contains citrulline, an amino acid that can help improve blood flow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The potassium in muskmelon helps maintain the body's electrolyte balance, which is crucial for cardiovascular function.</p>\n\n<h2>Vitamins and Nutrients</h2>\n<p>If you're looking to load up on essential vitamins, muskmelon is a great choice. It is rich in Vitamin A and Vitamin C, two crucial vitamins for overall health.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Vitamin A is essential for healthy vision, immune function, and skin health. Muskmelon is a fantastic source of beta-carotene, which is converted into vitamin A in the body.</li>\n  <li>Vitamin C is a powerful antioxidant that boosts the immune system, promotes healthy skin, and aids in iron absorption.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>While watermelon also contains these vitamins, muskmelon generally has higher concentrations, making it a more potent source of these nutrients.</p>\n\n<h2>Disease Prevention</h2>\n<p>Watermelon shines when it comes to fighting disease. Its deep red color reveals its high lycopene content, one of the most potent antioxidants found in nature. Lycopene has been linked to a reduced risk of certain diseases and improved heart health.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Watermelon's exceptional lycopene content makes it a top choice for long-term disease prevention.</li>\n  <li>Muskmelon contains other antioxidants, such as beta-carotene, but watermelon's lycopene content sets it apart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ultimately, the choice between watermelon and muskmelon isn't about which one is \"good\" and which is \"bad.\" Both are incredibly healthy, and the best approach is to enjoy a variety of both to get the full spectrum of vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants they have to offer.</p>\n\n<p>By incorporating watermelon and muskmelon into your diet, you can reap the benefits of their unique nutrients and antioxidants.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":true,"id":16}
{"id":5,"title":"Beyond the Diagnosis: How Daily Habits Shape Hormonal Health and Fertility","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) has become a widely recognized hormonal condition affecting individuals during their reproductive years. Many discover it in their twenties or early thirties when symptoms such as irregular cycles, unexplained weight changes, persistent acne, hair thinning, or difficulty conceiving prompt a closer look. While genetic predisposition plays a role, mounting evidence points to lifestyle factors — particularly chronic stress, disrupted sleep patterns, and prolonged sedentary behavior — as significant influencers of hormonal balance and fertility outcomes. Understanding these connections offers a path toward proactive, everyday management.</span></p>\n\n<h2>The Stress–Hormone Link: More Than Just Feeling Overwhelmed</h2>\n\n<p>Chronic stress triggers sustained elevation of cortisol, the body’s primary stress hormone. Elevated cortisol can interfere with the delicate feedback loop between the brain and the ovaries, potentially disrupting ovulation and menstrual regularity. For those already managing PCOS, this added hormonal burden may worsen symptoms such as insulin resistance and androgen excess. </p>\n\n<p>Managing stress is not about eliminating it entirely — that is neither realistic nor necessary. Rather, it involves building regular practices that help regulate the nervous system. Evidence-based approaches include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Mindfulness and meditation:</b> Even ten minutes of daily practice can lower cortisol levels over time.</li>\n  <li><b>Gentle movement:</b> Yoga, stretching, or walking outdoors shifts the body from a stress response toward a restorative state.</li>\n  <li><b>Structured downtime:</b> Scheduling non-negotiable periods for rest, hobbies, or social connection helps buffer cumulative stress.</li>\n  <li><b>Professional support:</b> Therapy or counseling provides tools for managing chronic stress and its physiological effects.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The goal is consistency, not perfection. Small, repeated actions build resilience and support hormonal stability.</p>\n\n<h2>Sleep and Circadian Rhythms: The Overlooked Pillar of Hormonal Health</h2>\n\n<p>Sleep is not a luxury — it is a biological necessity for hormonal regulation. During deep sleep, the body repairs tissues, balances appetite-regulating hormones, and processes cortisol. Inadequate or irregular sleep disrupts these processes, contributing to insulin resistance, increased inflammation, and worsened PCOS symptoms.</p>\n\n<p>Many women in their twenties and thirties struggle with inconsistent sleep due to demanding work schedules, social commitments, or screen exposure late into the night. Over time, this creates a cycle where poor sleep impairs metabolic health, and metabolic dysfunction further disrupts sleep.</p>\n\n<p>To protect hormonal rhythms, prioritize:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Consistent bed and wake times</b> — even on weekends — to anchor your circadian clock.</li>\n  <li><b>7–8 hours of sleep per night</b> as a minimum target for most adults.</li>\n  <li><b>Reduced screen exposure</b> 60 minutes before bed; blue light suppresses melatonin production.</li>\n  <li><b>A cool, dark, quiet sleep environment</b> to support deep, restorative rest.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Sedentary Work and Metabolic Health: The Desk Job Factor</h2>\n\n<p>Prolonged sitting — common in many modern work settings — reduces the muscles' ability to clear glucose from the bloodstream effectively. Over time, this contributes to insulin resistance, a core feature of PCOS for many women. When cells stop responding properly to insulin, the body compensates by producing more, which in turn stimulates the ovaries to produce excess androgens, worsening symptoms like acne, hair thinning, and irregular ovulation.</p>\n\n<p>The solution is not to quit desk work but to interrupt prolonged sitting with regular movement. Even brief, frequent breaks improve insulin sensitivity:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Stand or walk</b> for two to three minutes every hour.</li>\n  <li><b>Incorporate strength training</b> two to three times per week; muscle tissue is a major site for glucose disposal.</li>\n  <li><b>Add brisk walking</b> or other moderate aerobic activity for 150 minutes per week.</li>\n  <li><b>Use a standing desk</b> or take walking meetings when possible.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>These strategies do not require a gym membership or hours of free time — they fit into existing routines with intentional planning.</p>\n\n<h2>Early Recognition: Why Cycle Irregularity Deserves Attention</h2>\n\n<p>A common pattern among those later diagnosed with PCOS is that irregular periods in the early twenties were dismissed as “just how things are” or expected to settle with time. While occasional cycle variation is normal, persistent irregularity — cycles consistently shorter than 21 days or longer than 35 days, or missing periods altogether — warrants evaluation. Early diagnosis opens the door to timely lifestyle adjustments, metabolic monitoring, and, if needed, ovulation support, all of which improve long-term fertility prospects and overall health.</p>\n\n<p>If you experience any of the following, consider consulting a healthcare professional:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Fewer than eight menstrual cycles per year</li>\n  <li>Cycles that vary widely month to month</li>\n  <li>Excessive hair growth on the face, chest, or back</li>\n  <li>Severe acne or male-pattern hair thinning</li>\n  <li>Difficulty achieving pregnancy after six to twelve months of trying</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Evidence-Based Lifestyle Strategies for Managing PCOS</h2>\n\n<p>The encouraging news is that lifestyle modification can meaningfully improve outcomes for many women with PCOS. Research supports several targeted approaches:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Modest weight reduction:</b> For those who are overweight, a loss of 5–10% of total body weight can restore ovulation and improve insulin sensitivity in a significant proportion of women.</li>\n  <li><b>Structured physical activity:</b> Combining aerobic exercise with resistance training yields the greatest improvements in metabolic health and hormonal balance.</li>\n  <li><b>Stress management techniques:</b> Yoga, mindfulness-based stress reduction, and cognitive behavioral therapy have all shown benefits for reducing cortisol and improving cycle regularity.</li>\n  <li><b>Sleep hygiene:</b> Prioritizing consistent, adequate sleep supports cortisol regulation and insulin function.</li>\n  <li><b>Nutritional adjustments:</b> Emphasizing whole foods, fiber-rich carbohydrates, lean proteins, and healthy fats while reducing ultra-processed foods and added sugars can help stabilize blood glucose and reduce androgen levels.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>No single approach works for everyone, and individual needs vary. Working with a healthcare provider — such as a gynecologist, endocrinologist, or registered dietitian — can help tailor these strategies to your specific situation.</p>\n\n<h2>Fertility in Context: Metabolic Health Matters at Every Age</h2>\n\n<p>Fertility in the twenties and thirties is influenced not only by age but also by metabolic health. PCOS is a manageable condition, but it requires awareness and consistent action. The daily choices you make — how you manage stress, when you sleep, how you move, and what you eat — collectively shape your hormonal environment and, ultimately, your reproductive health. Small, deliberate changes made today can improve ovulation and conception outcomes, while also supporting long-term well-being.</p>\n\n<p><i>This article is for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for guidance specific to your health situation.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/P-H-V/FEVE-images/main/article-images/1790195615455_Beyond-the-Diagnosis.png","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":6,"title":"Essential Nutrition Guide for Women: Tips for Lifelong Health!","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Le Women around the world often juggle multiple roles—caring for families, advancing careers, and managing households. In the midst of these demands, personal nutrition frequently takes a back seat. Yet a woman’s health is not only vital for her own well-being but also forms the foundation of her family’s vitality. Prioritizing the right nutrition is about more than fitting into a certain size—it’s about sustaining energy, supporting long-term health, and empowering yourself to thrive at every stage of life.</span></p>\n\n<p>The following principles are rooted in nutritional science and designed to be adaptable across cultures, dietary preferences, and life stages. These are not rigid rules but flexible guidelines to help you build a nourishing, balanced relationship with food.</p>\n\n<h3>1. Prioritize Protein at Every Meal</h3>\n<p>Protein is essential for maintaining muscle strength, supporting hormone production, and aiding recovery from daily stress or exercise. Including a source of protein—such as legumes, eggs, dairy products, fish, tofu, or lean meats—at each meal helps stabilize blood sugar, keeps you full longer, and provides the building blocks your body needs to function optimally.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> Women often consume less protein than recommended, especially during busy periods. Aim for roughly 20–30 grams per meal, adjusting for activity level and life stage.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Make Iron a Daily Priority</h3>\n<p>Iron deficiency is one of the most widespread nutritional concerns among women globally, contributing to fatigue, weakness, and reduced immunity. Menstruation, pregnancy, and postpartum recovery increase iron needs. Regular intake of iron-rich foods—such as leafy greens, lentils, beans, fortified cereals, and dried fruits like apricots or dates—can help maintain healthy levels. Pair these with vitamin C sources (e.g., citrus, bell peppers) to enhance absorption.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> If you suspect deficiency (persistent tiredness, pale skin, brittle nails), consult a healthcare professional before self-supplementing, as excess iron can be harmful.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Support Bone Health with Calcium and Vitamin D</h3>\n<p>Bone density peaks in early adulthood and gradually declines, with rapid loss during menopause. Calcium and vitamin D work in tandem to maintain skeletal strength. Good calcium sources include dairy products, fortified plant milks, leafy greens, and seeds like sesame. Vitamin D is primarily obtained from sunlight exposure, but many people benefit from fortified foods or a supplement, especially during darker months.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> Weight-bearing exercise (walking, dancing, resistance training) further strengthens bones and complements a calcium-rich diet.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Embrace Healthy Fats for Hormonal Balance</h3>\n<p>Fats are not the enemy—certain types are crucial for hormone regulation, reproductive health, and brain function. Include unsaturated fats from nuts, seeds, avocados, olive oil, and omega-3-rich foods like flaxseeds, chia seeds, or fatty fish. These fats also help absorb fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K).</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> Omega-3 fatty acids, in particular, have been shown to support mood stability and reduce inflammation, offering both physical and mental benefits.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Stay Hydrated—Every Cell Depends on It</h3>\n<p>Water influences digestion, metabolism, skin health, and cognitive performance. Even mild dehydration can trigger fatigue, headaches, and difficulty concentrating. While individual needs vary, a good target is roughly 8 cups (2 liters) daily from beverages and water-rich foods like fruits and vegetables. Increase intake during exercise, hot weather, or if you’re breastfeeding.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> Herbal teas, infused water, and soups can contribute to hydration while adding variety without added sugar.</p>\n\n<h3>6. Eat at Regular Intervals, Don’t Skip Meals</h3>\n<p>Irregular eating patterns can disrupt metabolism, lead to energy crashes, and cause blood sugar fluctuations that affect mood and focus. Structured, balanced meals at consistent times help maintain steady energy and prevent overeating later. Aim for three meals plus one or two snacks if needed, tailored to your schedule and hunger cues.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> Skipping breakfast or lunch often leads to excessive hunger later, making it harder to choose nutritious options.</p>\n\n<h3>7. Increase Fiber for Gut and Overall Health</h3>\n<p>A fiber-rich diet (whole grains, fruits, vegetables, legumes, nuts, seeds) supports healthy digestion, feeds beneficial gut bacteria, and helps regulate cholesterol and blood sugar. Emerging research also links a healthy gut microbiome to improved immunity and mental well-being. Aim for at least 25–30 grams of fiber daily, increasing gradually to avoid discomfort.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> Fiber also promotes satiety, which can aid in weight management without restrictive dieting.</p>\n\n<h3>8. Reduce Ultra-Processed Foods</h3>\n<p>Convenience foods often contain high levels of added sugars, unhealthy fats, sodium, and artificial additives. Frequent consumption has been linked to inflammation, hormonal imbalances, and chronic conditions such as heart disease and type 2 diabetes. While convenience is sometimes necessary, prioritizing fresh, minimally processed meals—even simple ones—significantly improves nutritional quality.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> You don’t need to eliminate all processed foods; focus on reducing the most highly processed items (sugary drinks, packaged snacks, fast food) and replacing them with whole-food alternatives.</p>\n\n<h3>9. Nourish Your Mental Well-Being</h3>\n<p>Deficiencies in key nutrients—such as iron, B vitamins, vitamin D, magnesium, and omega-3 fatty acids—can negatively affect mood, focus, and emotional resilience. A balanced diet supports brain chemistry and helps manage stress. Incorporating a variety of colorful vegetables, lean proteins, healthy fats, and whole grains provides the spectrum of micronutrients your brain needs.</p>\n<p><b>Insight:</b> Mindful eating—paying attention to hunger cues, eating without distraction, and savoring flavors—can enhance both digestion and satisfaction.</p>\n\n<h2>Why Nutrition Matters More Than Ever</h2>\n<p>Think of nutrition not as a quick fix for existing problems, but as a lifelong investment in your vitality. Many health conditions—from bone loss to heart disease—develop gradually, and a nourishing diet is one of the most powerful tools to reduce risk and maintain wellness. Small, consistent choices (a handful of nuts instead of chips, an extra glass of water, a side of vegetables) add up over time.</p>\n<p>In a world that constantly demands your energy, prioritizing your own nutrition is an act of self-care and empowerment. When you feel your best, you’re better equipped to care for others and pursue your own goals. Start with one or two tips that resonate, and build from there. Your body—and your future self—will thank you.</p><p><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/P-H-V/FEVE-images/main/article-images/1790195605166_Essential-Nutrition-Guide.png","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":3,"title":"3 hidden pregnancy risks most women don’t realise","content":"<h2>The Overlooked Risks in Pregnancy: Small Habits That Matter</h2>\n\n<p>Preventive care during pregnancy is widely understood in principle: regular check-ups, prenatal supplements, and timely scans. On paper, it all appears complete. Yet much of a woman’s pregnancy health is shaped between these visits. Small, everyday patterns tend to go unnoticed. Routine reports look fine, appointments are spaced out, and there is a quiet reassurance that things are on track. At the same time, certain habits slip through — sometimes out of routine, sometimes out of convenience. This is where preventive care begins to thin out, even when intentions are right.</p>\n\n<p>The reality is that pregnancy health is not defined only by clinical markers. It is influenced by the in-between moments — the skipped meals, the hurried workdays, the restless nights. These patterns often build gradually, and the body registers them long before they show up in a test result.</p>\n\n<h2>What Are the Most Common Concerns?</h2>\n\n<p>In pregnancy, as with many health matters, it is easy to miss subtle clues when everyday tasks become routine. According to a women’s health specialist, prenatal supplements are often taken regularly, but meals may be delayed or skipped — especially on busy workdays. Rest gets adjusted around deadlines, while hydration becomes an afterthought. None of this is deliberate neglect. It builds gradually: one missed meal, one late night, one postponed break. Over time, these small compromises begin to add up. Routine reports may not immediately reflect this, making it easy to overlook — but the body often registers these patterns early.</p>\n\n<p>Beyond nutrition and rest, other subtle factors frequently go unaddressed:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Stress and mental load</b> — Chronic low-level stress can affect blood pressure and sleep quality, yet it is rarely discussed as a pregnancy risk.</li>\n  <li><b>Inconsistent hydration</b> — Even mild dehydration can contribute to headaches, fatigue, and changes in uterine blood flow.</li>\n  <li><b>Sleep disruption</b> — Interrupted sleep is common, but persistent poor sleep has been linked to higher risks of gestational diabetes and hypertension.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What Are the Hidden Risks?</h2>\n\n<p>Pregnancy does come with some discomfort, and it is not unusual for this to be normalized. Mild swelling, fatigue, and occasional headaches are often brushed aside as expected changes. And this is where things can be missed. Conditions such as <b>gestational hypertension</b> (high blood pressure during pregnancy) may begin with subtle signs that are easily overlooked. Similarly, <b>anemia</b> (low hemoglobin levels) can present as tiredness that blends into daily routine. When symptoms do not disrupt daily functioning, they are often allowed to pass without much thought. Awareness exists, but action often gets delayed.</p>\n\n<p>Another overlooked risk is the impact of irregular eating patterns on blood sugar stability. Skipping meals or going long hours without food can lead to drops in glucose that affect energy and mood, and may contribute to nausea or dizziness. For some women, this pattern can also increase the likelihood of excessive weight gain later in pregnancy as the body compensates.</p>\n\n<p>Additionally, <b>urinary tract infections</b> (UTIs) deserve attention. They are common during pregnancy but can present with subtle signs like mild discomfort or changes in urine color. Left untreated, a UTI can progress and affect kidney function or increase the risk of preterm labor.</p>\n\n<h2>What to Watch For</h2>\n\n<p>It is important not to wait for symptoms to become disruptive before seeking advice. When unsure, it is better to check early than to wait for more clarity. Pay attention to changes that persist:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Fatigue that does not improve with rest</li>\n  <li>Swelling in the hands, face, or feet that worsens</li>\n  <li>Headaches that are frequent or do not resolve with hydration or usual care</li>\n  <li>Changes in urination (frequency, discomfort, or odor)</li>\n  <li>Persistent low mood, irritability, or difficulty concentrating</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Practical Steps That Help</h2>\n\n<p>The following strategies, while simple, can make a meaningful difference in pregnancy outcomes:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Prioritize regular meals and hydration</b> — These matter as much as prescribed supplements. Aim to eat every 3–4 hours and drink water throughout the day.</li>\n  <li><b>Build rest into your schedule</b> — Short breaks during the day can reduce cumulative fatigue. Even 10 minutes of lying down or deep breathing helps.</li>\n  <li><b>Track subtle symptoms</b> — Keep a simple journal or note on your phone. If fatigue, swelling, or headaches recur, mention them at your next visit.</li>\n  <li><b>Follow up proactively</b> — Routine appointments are not formalities; they track changes that may not be obvious. Bring your observations to each visit.</li>\n  <li><b>Ask about mental well-being</b> — Emotional health is a vital part of pregnancy care. If you feel stressed, anxious, or down, share this with your healthcare provider.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The Bigger Picture</h2>\n\n<p>A healthy pregnancy is not defined only by reports or scheduled visits. It is shaped quietly in the in-between moments. Often, it is the smaller decisions — waiting for advice versus seeking it immediately — that influence how smoothly things unfold throughout the pregnancy. As one obstetrician put it: “The body registers patterns long before they appear on a report. Listening to those early signals is one of the most powerful tools a woman has.”</p>\n\n<p>No pregnancy is free of worry, but awareness of these hidden risks empowers women to act early. By paying attention to everyday habits and subtle changes, you can take an active role in safeguarding both your health and your baby’s well-being.</p><p><br></p>\n\n<p><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"/articles/article-images/1779414174_jeanetteatherton-pregnant-woman-7909896_640.jpg","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/P-H-V/FEVE-images/main/article-images/1789948947897_article-1.png","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":true}
{"title":"The Fourth Trimester: Why Postpartum Care Demands More Attention","img":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/P-H-V/FEVE-images/main/article-images/1790128226154_Beyond-the-Diagnosis.png","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">In The moment a baby is born, the spotlight shifts almost entirely to the newborn. The mother, who has just completed one of the most physically and emotionally demanding experiences of her life, quietly moves to the background. This is the fourth trimester — the 12 weeks after childbirth — and for millions of women worldwide, it remains the most underserved chapter of maternal healthcare. Physical recovery is visible, but the emotional weight a new mother carries is rarely spoken about, let alone addressed.</span></p>\n\n<h3>The Hidden Emotional Challenge</h3>\n\n<p>Managing a newborn’s constant needs, recovering from birth, navigating a new identity, and often returning to household responsibilities creates a compounding emotional load that builds silently. Postpartum depression affects up to one in five new mothers globally, yet it remains significantly underdiagnosed. Cultural norms in many societies frame all discomfort as normal, leading women to spend months pushing through experiences that genuinely warrant professional attention.</p>\n\n<p>Emotional exhaustion and social withdrawal often go unnoticed because symptoms do not always appear as people expect. Persistent sadness, tearfulness, or feeling disconnected from the baby should never be dismissed as just “baby blues.”</p>\n\n<h3>Physical Signs That Need Attention</h3>\n\n<p>Postpartum recovery involves the whole body. Several signs point to a need for proper medical support:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Persistent sadness, tearfulness, or feeling disconnected from the baby</li>\n  <li>Extreme fatigue that does not improve with rest</li>\n  <li>Pelvic pain, urinary leakage, or pressure in the lower abdomen</li>\n  <li>Wound discomfort or delayed healing after a cesarean section</li>\n  <li>Difficulty concentrating, anxiety, or intrusive thoughts</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Components of Comprehensive Postpartum Care</h3>\n\n<p>A well-structured postpartum plan goes well beyond a single six-week check-up. It should actively address:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Lactation support:</b> Breastfeeding difficulties are common and often go unsupported; early, consistent guidance improves outcomes for both mother and baby.</li>\n  <li><b>Postpartum exercise:</b> Gradual, guided movements help restore core strength and pelvic floor function and should be introduced at an appropriate pace under proper supervision.</li>\n  <li><b>Vaccination:</b> Both the mother and the newborn have specific immunization needs in the postpartum period that health professionals often overlook during routine follow-up.</li>\n  <li><b>Contraception counseling:</b> Family planning should be included in early postpartum consultations rather than left to chance or deferred to a later visit.</li>\n  <li><b>Nutrition planning:</b> A structured diet addressing post-pregnancy depletion of iron, calcium, and essential vitamins supports faster, more complete healing.</li>\n  <li><b>Returning to pre-pregnancy weight and shape:</b> This is a real, valid concern for most new mothers. With the right guidance on safe timelines, progressive exercise, and balanced nutrition, it is an achievable goal without pressure or unrealistic expectations.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Special Considerations for Assisted Pregnancies</h3>\n\n<p>For women who have conceived through assisted reproductive technologies such as in vitro fertilization (IVF), the need for structured postpartum follow-up is even greater. These pregnancies often involve higher physical and emotional stakes, making ongoing maternal care not just beneficial but essential. The fourth trimester in these cases demands a more personalized, closely monitored care plan.</p>\n\n<h3>Conclusion: A Call for More Support</h3>\n\n<p>The fourth trimester is not a footnote to pregnancy. It warrants the same clinical attention, structured planning, and consistent support as the nine months that preceded it. Every new mother deserves a care system that recognizes her recovery, validates her emotional experience, and provides the resources she needs to thrive — not just survive — after childbirth.</p><p><br></p>\n\n<p><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended solely for informational purposes and should not be considered a substitute for professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized advice regarding your specific medical condition or concerns.</i></p>","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":true,"id":17}
{"id":7,"title":"The Hidden Dangers of Ultra-Processed Foods: Protecting Your Heart and Health","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Ultra-processed foods—such as instant noodles, sugary drinks, packaged snacks, and ready-made meals—are often inexpensive and convenient. However, health experts warn that relying on them regularly can pose serious risks to your heart and overall well-being. These industrially manufactured products typically contain additives, artificial flavors, and high levels of sugar, salt, and unhealthy fats to enhance taste and extend shelf life. What was once an occasional treat has become a daily staple for many people around the world.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\"><br></span></p>\n\n<p>A growing body of research links high consumption of ultra-processed foods to an increased risk of heart disease. A report in a leading cardiology journal suggests that these foods may contribute to cardiovascular problems over time. Similarly, a large-scale study published in a major medical journal found that individuals who derived about one-fifth of their daily calories from ultra-processed foods had a significantly higher chance of developing type 2 diabetes compared to those who consumed roughly half that amount. These foods are also associated with weight gain, high blood pressure, and unhealthy cholesterol levels—all of which are risk factors for heart disease.</p><p><br></p>\n\n<p>A diet based on whole, natural foods is far better for heart health. Yet low prices, busy schedules, and powerful marketing make ultra-processed options hard to resist. Fresh fruits, vegetables, and home-cooked meals often seem more time-consuming and expensive, leading many to skip them in favor of quick alternatives.</p>\n\n<h2>Key Ways Ultra-Processed Foods Harm Your Health</h2>\n\n<p>Ultra-processed foods are typically high in added sugars, unhealthy fats, and refined grains while being low in essential nutrients like fiber, vitamins, and minerals. Common examples include packaged snacks, sugary breakfast cereals, sodas, and fast food. According to heart health organizations, regular consumption can lead to obesity, heart disease, diabetes, and other chronic conditions.</p>\n\n<p>Nutritionists highlight several mechanisms through which ultra-processed foods damage heart health and overall well-being:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Excess sugar, salt, and unhealthy fats</b> contribute to weight gain and increase the risk of insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes.</li>\n  <li><b>Harm to gut health</b> can trigger chronic inflammation, which is closely linked to heart disease.</li>\n  <li><b>High sodium and trans fats</b> directly harm blood vessels and raise blood pressure.</li>\n  <li><b>Unfavorable cholesterol changes</b>—these foods can raise harmful LDL cholesterol while lowering protective HDL cholesterol.</li>\n  <li><b>Hyper-palatability</b> makes these foods difficult to resist. They are engineered to taste very good, which can override natural fullness cues and lead to overeating and frequent snacking.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Why Do We Eat So Many Processed Foods?</h2>\n\n<p>The food environment plays a major role. Ultra-processed products are heavily marketed, widely available, and often cheaper per calorie than fresh alternatives. Busy lifestyles leave little time for cooking, and many people lack access to affordable fresh produce. Additionally, these foods are designed to be addictive—their combination of sugar, salt, and fat triggers reward centers in the brain, making it easy to eat more than intended.</p>\n\n<h2>Practical Steps to Reduce Ultra-Processed Foods</h2>\n\n<p>Making changes doesn’t have to be overwhelming. Small shifts can significantly improve your diet:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Cook at home more often</b>—even simple meals like soups, stir-fries, or salads can be quick and nourishing.</li>\n  <li><b>Read ingredient labels</b>—look for products with fewer ingredients and recognizable names. Avoid items with long lists of additives and preservatives.</li>\n  <li><b>Choose whole snacks</b>—swap chips and cookies for fruits, nuts, yogurt, or vegetable sticks.</li>\n  <li><b>Drink water or unsweetened beverages</b> instead of sodas or sugary juices.</li>\n  <li><b>Plan ahead</b>—preparing meals or snacks in advance reduces the temptation to grab processed options when hungry.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Ultra-Processed Foods</h2>\n\n<h3>What exactly are ultra-processed foods?</h3>\n<p>Ultra-processed foods are industrial formulations made mostly from substances extracted from whole foods, such as oils, sugars, starches, and proteins. They often contain additives like artificial flavors, colors, preservatives, and emulsifiers. Examples include instant noodles, sodas, packaged chips, frozen pizza, and many breakfast cereals.</p>\n\n<h3>How do they impact heart health?</h3>\n<p>They can lead to weight gain, high blood pressure, unhealthy cholesterol levels, and chronic inflammation—all major risk factors for heart disease. Over time, a diet high in ultra-processed foods increases the likelihood of developing cardiovascular conditions.</p>\n\n<h3>Why are these foods so common in modern diets?</h3>\n<p>Low cost, convenience, aggressive marketing, and the fast pace of daily life make ultra-processed foods an easy choice. Healthier options like fresh fruits, vegetables, and home-cooked meals often require more time, money, and planning.</p>\n\n<h3>Can ultra-processed foods be addictive?</h3>\n<p>Yes, many are engineered to be hyper-palatable—combining high levels of sugar, fat, and salt that stimulate pleasure centers in the brain. This can override the body’s natural fullness signals, leading to overeating and a cycle of cravings.</p>\n\n<h3>Is it necessary to eliminate them completely?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. The goal is to reduce the proportion of ultra-processed foods in your diet, not to eliminate them entirely. Prioritizing whole, minimally processed foods most of the time can greatly benefit your health. Occasional treats are fine as part of a balanced lifestyle.</p><p><br></p>\n\n<p><i>Disclaimer: This article is for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for guidance specific to your health situation.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":8,"title":"Understanding Vitamin D Deficiency: 10 Key Factors to Consider","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Vitamin D plays a crucial role in maintaining strong bones, a healthy immune system, and overall well-being. Despite its importance, many individuals experience low vitamin D levels, even when they spend time outdoors and take supplements. This deficiency can be attributed to various factors beyond sunlight exposure, including digestion, stress, diet, and gut health.</span></p>\n\n<h3>Factors Contributing to Low Vitamin D Levels</h3>\n<p>It is essential to recognize that vitamin D deficiency can have significant implications for overall health, affecting bone strength, immunity, muscle health, and mood regulation. The following factors may contribute to low vitamin D levels:</p>\n\n<h3>1. Inadequate Magnesium Intake</h3>\n<p>Magnesium is necessary for activating vitamin D in the body. Without sufficient magnesium, vitamin D may remain inactive. Incorporating magnesium-rich foods into your diet can help, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leafy greens like spinach</li>\n<li>Nuts and seeds, such as almonds and pumpkin seeds</li>\n<li.dark chocolate<=\"\" li=\"\">\n</li.dark></ul>\n\n<h3>2. Incorrect Supplement Timing</h3>\n<p>Vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin, requiring fat for proper absorption. Taking supplements on an empty stomach may reduce absorption and cause discomfort. It is recommended to take vitamin D with healthy fats, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eggs</li>\n<li>Yogurt</li>\n<li>Nuts and seeds</li>\n<li>Avocado</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Poor Bile Flow and Digestion</h3>\n<p>Bile production is essential for digesting fats and absorbing fat-soluble vitamins like vitamin D. Supporting digestion with healthy fats, fiber-rich foods, and adequate hydration can help maintain optimal bile flow.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Vitamin K2 Deficiency</h3>\n<p>Vitamin D helps absorb calcium, but vitamin K2 is necessary for directing calcium into bones rather than arteries. Foods rich in vitamin K2 include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cheese</li>\n<li>Egg yolks</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supplements should only be taken under medical guidance.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Incorrect Vitamin D Dosage</h3>\n<p>Taking very low or high doses of vitamin D without consulting a healthcare professional can be ineffective. General recommendations suggest:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infants: 400 IU</li>\n<li>Children and adults: 600 IU</li>\n<li>Older adults: 800 IU</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Getting vitamin D levels tested before starting supplements is crucial.</p>\n\n<h3>6. Chronic Stress</h3>\n<p>Long-term stress can increase inflammation, affecting hormone regulation and vitamin D processing. Managing stress through:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regular exercise</li>\n<li>Improved sleep</li>\n<li>Meditation</li>\n<li>Outdoor activities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>can help mitigate its impact on vitamin D levels.</p>\n\n<h3>7. Limited Sunlight Exposure</h3>\n<pwhile sunlight=\"\" is=\"\" essential=\"\" for=\"\" vitamin=\"\" d=\"\" production,=\"\" factors=\"\" like=\"\" pollution,=\"\" sunscreen,=\"\" clothing,=\"\" and=\"\" limited=\"\" outdoor=\"\" time=\"\" can=\"\" reduce=\"\" its=\"\" effectiveness.=\"\" experts=\"\" recommend=\"\" 10-30=\"\" minutes=\"\" of=\"\" midday=\"\" sunlight,=\"\" depending=\"\" on=\"\" skin=\"\" type=\"\" weather=\"\" conditions.<=\"\" p=\"\">\n\n<h3>8. Gut Health Issues</h3>\n<p>Digestive disorders, such as celiac disease, IBS, and Crohn's disease, can impede nutrient absorption, including vitamin D. Improving gut health and addressing underlying conditions may help increase vitamin D levels.</p>\n\n<h3>9. Obesity</h3>\n<p>Excess body fat can trap vitamin D, reducing its availability for the body. Maintaining a healthy weight may help improve vitamin D levels and overall well-being.</p>\n\n<h3>10. Underlying Medical Conditions</h3>\n<p>Certain medical conditions, such as liver or kidney disease, can affect the body's ability to convert vitamin D into its active form. Regular monitoring and consultation with a healthcare professional are essential for managing vitamin D levels in these cases.</p>\n\n<p>Remember, vitamin D deficiency can have significant implications for overall health. By understanding the factors that contribute to low vitamin D levels, individuals can take proactive steps to maintain optimal levels and support their well-being.</p><p><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p></pwhile>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":9,"title":"Meeting Your Daily Protein Needs on a Vegetarian Diet","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">As a vegetarian, you may have encountered the question, \"Where do you get your protein from?\" numerous times. However, with a little planning and understanding of your food options, it's easy to meet your daily protein requirements. Protein is essential for muscle strength, energy, immunity, and recovery, making it a crucial part of a balanced diet.</span></p>\n\n<h2>Understanding Your Protein Needs</h2>\n<p>The daily protein requirement varies based on factors such as weight, activity level, and overall health. Generally, the minimum requirement is 0.8g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day. For example, a person weighing 70kg would require at least 56g of protein per day. For those with moderate or active lifestyles, the recommended intake is 1.2-1.3g/kg and 1.5-1.6g/kg, respectively.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimum requirement: 0.8g protein per kg body weight/day</li>\n<li>Moderate activity: 1.2–1.3 g/kg</li>\n<li>Active lifestyle: 1.5–1.6 g/kg</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Aiming for 20-40g of protein per meal can support better absorption and muscle health. While higher intake up to 2g per kg is generally safe, exceeding this amount regularly may not offer additional benefits.</p>\n\n<h2>Best Vegetarian Protein Sources</h2>\n<p>A balanced vegetarian diet should include a mix of protein-rich foods from various categories, including:</p>\n<h3>Dairy Options</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Paneer (100g): 18-20g protein</li>\n<li>Greek yogurt (100g): 10.5g protein</li>\n<li>Milk (1 cup): 8g protein</li>\n<li>Cheese (28g): 7g protein</li>\n<li>Soy milk (250ml): 7g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Legumes and Pulses</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Lentils (100g): 24-26g protein</li>\n<li>Chickpeas (100g): 19-20g protein</li>\n<li>Rajma (100g): 23-24g protein</li>\n<li>Soybeans (100g): 36-40g protein</li>\n<li>Edamame (100g): 35-36g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Pair legumes and pulses with cereal for amino acid balance.</p>\n<h3>Soy Products</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Tofu (100g): 10-12g protein</li>\n<li>Soy chunks (25g dry): 13-14g protein</li>\n<li>Sprouts (moong, chana) (100g): 6-8g protein</li>\n<li>Hummus (100g): 7.5g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Grains and Cereals</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Quinoa (100g): 14g protein</li>\n<li>Oats (50g): 6-8g protein</li>\n<li>Brown rice (100g): 7.2g protein</li>\n<li>Millet (100g): 8-10g protein</li>\n<li>Barley (jau) (100g): 9g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: These are carbohydrate-dominant foods with modest protein, best paired with legumes and pulses to improve amino acid balance.</p>\n<h3>Vegetables</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Peas (100g): 5-6g protein</li>\n<li>Broccoli (100g): 3g protein</li>\n<li>Mushrooms (100g): 3g protein</li>\n<li>Sweet corn (100g): 3g protein</li>\n<li>Brussels sprouts (100g): 4g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Nuts, Seeds, and Extras</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Peanuts (100g): 25.8g protein</li>\n<li>Peanut butter (2 tbsp): 7g protein</li>\n<li>Chia seeds (1 tbsp): 2-2.5g protein</li>\n<li>Hemp seeds (1 tbsp): 3.5-4g protein</li>\n<li>Sunflower seeds (1 tbsp): 2g protein</li>\n<li>Roasted chana (30g): 6g protein</li>\n<li>Mixed nuts (¼ cup): 7g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember to portion these calorie-dense foods: stick to 1 tbsp of seeds at a time and 5 pieces of nuts.</p>\n\n<h2>Reaching Your Daily Protein Goals</h2>\n<p>Hitting 50g of protein can be simple with basic meals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breakfast: Oats + milk + chia seeds: 14-16g protein</li>\n<li>Lunch: Dal + roti + curd: 18-20g protein</li>\n<li>Snack: Roasted chana + nuts: 10-12g protein</li>\n<li>Dinner: Paneer + vegetables: 10-12g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This combination can give you around 50-55g of protein without complicated planning.</p>\n<p>For higher goals, slightly upgrade your meals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breakfast: Besan chilla + Greek yogurt: 25-30g protein</li>\n<li>Snack: Soy milk + seeds: 15-20g protein</li>\n<li>Lunch: Rajma + rice + paneer + curd: 30-35g protein</li>\n<li>Evening: Roasted chana: 10-12g protein</li>\n<li>Dinner: Tofu or soy chunks: 20-25g protein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This plan can help you reach 95-110g of protein comfortably.</p>\n\n<h2>Why Protein Combinations Matter</h2>\n<p>Unlike animal protein, most plant proteins are incomplete, meaning they lack some essential amino acids. Pairing foods like dal with rice or roti with legumes helps create a complete protein profile. A balanced protein intake spread across meals improves muscle protein synthesis more effectively than consuming it all at once.</p>\n\n<h2>Tips to Improve Protein Absorption</h2>\n<p>To boost protein absorption, remember to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Space protein intake every 3-4 hours</li>\n<li>Chew food properly and eat slowly</li>\n<li>Include yoghurt and fermented foods for gut health</li>\n<li>Add fruits like papaya and pineapple for digestive enzymes</li>\n<li>Manage stress and avoid overly processed foods</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Signs of Poor Protein Absorption</h2>\n<p>Watch out for signs like fatigue, hair fall, slow recovery, brittle nails, or frequent cravings. These may indicate poor absorption or low intake.</p><p><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":10,"title":"Nutritionist suggests 10 anti-inflammatory foods that may help prevent","content":"<h2>The Role of Nutrition in Reducing Chronic Inflammation</h2>\n<p>Chronic inflammation is a often overlooked factor in long-term health, playing a powerful role in shaping our overall wellbeing. While acute inflammation is a natural and helpful response to injury or infection, low-grade, ongoing inflammation can create an internal environment where dysfunction builds, increasing the risk of chronic conditions. This type of inflammation may not cause obvious symptoms, but its effects can be profound.</p>\n<p>Inflammation is a natural process that helps our bodies heal and repair damaged tissues. However, when this response becomes chronic, it can lead to a range of health problems. A key factor in managing chronic inflammation is a balanced diet, rich in whole foods and nutrients that support overall health.</p>\n\n<h2>Understanding Anti-Inflammatory Diets</h2>\n<p>An anti-inflammatory diet is not a short-term fix, but a long-term support system for the body. It is based on whole, nutrient-dense foods that provide the necessary building blocks for optimal health. This includes good-quality protein sources, such as lean meats, fish, and eggs, as well as healthy fats like nuts, seeds, and avocado.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Whole, unprocessed foods like vegetables, fruits, and whole grains provide essential vitamins, minerals, and antioxidants that help reduce oxidative stress and inflammation.</li>\n  <li>Healthy fats like omega-3 fatty acids, found in fatty fish, nuts, and seeds, also play a critical role in reducing inflammation.</li>\n  <li>Herbs and spices like turmeric, ginger, and garlic have potent anti-inflammatory properties and can be incorporated into daily meals for added benefit.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Key Principles of an Anti-Inflammatory Diet</h2>\n<p>An anti-inflammatory diet is not about restriction, but about developing an internal environment that supports overall health and wellbeing. It involves making informed food choices that promote balance and reduce inflammation. This includes:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Eating a variety of whole, unprocessed foods to provide essential nutrients and fiber.</li>\n  <li>Incorporating healthy fats and omega-3 rich foods to reduce inflammation.</li>\n  <li>Limiting or avoiding ultra-processed foods, refined sugars, and unhealthy fats that can fuel inflammation.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Additional Factors in Managing Inflammation</h2>\n<p>While diet plays a critical role in managing chronic inflammation, it is just one part of the picture. Other essential factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Maintaining a healthy gut microbiome through a balanced diet and probiotics.</li>\n  <li>Managing stress through relaxation techniques, such as meditation or yoga.</li>\n  <li>Getting adequate sleep and prioritizing overall wellbeing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By incorporating these principles into daily life, individuals can reduce their risk of chronic inflammation and promote overall health and wellbeing.</p><p><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":11,"title":"Why drinking water alone isn't enough? Role of electrolytes explained by a dietitian","content":"<li></li>\n\n<li>Log Out</li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li>Log Out</li>\n\n<li>Switch to हिन्दी</li>\n\n<li>Healthy Eating Nutrition Recipes Superfoods</li>\n\n<li>Nutrition</li>\n\n<li>Recipes</li>\n\n<li>Superfoods</li>\n\n<li>Beauty Hair Care Natural Cures Skin Care</li>\n\n<li>Hair Care</li>\n\n<li>Natural Cures</li>\n\n<li>Skin Care</li>\n\n<li>Intimate Health Feminine Hygiene Menstruation Sexual Health</li>\n\n<li>Feminine Hygiene</li>\n\n<li>Menstruation</li>\n\n<li>Sexual Health</li>\n\n<li>Preventive Care Family Care Reproductive Care Self Care</li>\n\n<li>Family Care</li>\n\n<li>Reproductive Care</li>\n\n<li>Self Care</li>\n\n<li>How To</li>\n\n<li>Recommends</li>\n\n<li>Fitness Muscle Gain Staying Fit Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Muscle Gain</li>\n\n<li>Staying Fit</li>\n\n<li>Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Mind Emotional Health Happiness Hacks Mental Health</li>\n\n<li>Emotional Health</li>\n\n<li>Happiness Hacks</li>\n\n<li>Mental Health</li>\n\n<li>Brand Posts</li>\n\n<li>More Switch to हिन्दी Mind Daily Health Diseases Web Stories Health News She Slays Mom Says Horoscope Videos Podcasts Quizzes Quick Shots I Need Help Meet The Experts Rss Feed Partner With Us BMI Calculator About Us Personalise your feed Hindustan Times Live Hindustan Livemint</li>\n\n<li>Switch to हिन्दी</li>\n\n<li>Mind</li>\n\n<li>Daily Health</li>\n\n<li>Diseases</li>\n\n<li>Web Stories</li>\n\n<li>Health News</li>\n\n<li>She Slays</li>\n\n<li>Mom Says</li>\n\n<li>Horoscope</li>\n\n<li>Videos</li>\n\n<li>Podcasts</li>\n\n<li>Quizzes</li>\n\n<li>Quick Shots</li>\n\n<li>I Need Help</li>\n\n<li>Meet The Experts</li>\n\n<li>Rss Feed</li>\n\n<li>Partner With Us</li>\n\n<li>BMI Calculator</li>\n\n<li>About Us</li>\n\n<li>Personalise your feed</li>\n\n<li>Hindustan Times</li>\n\n<li>Live Hindustan</li>\n\n<li>Livemint</li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li>drinking water</li>\n\n<li>Nutrition</li>\n\n<li>Recipes</li>\n\n<li>Superfoods</li>\n\n<li>Women’s Health</li>\n\n<li>Healthy Eating</li>\n\n<li>Nutrition</li>\n\n<li>Why drinking water alone isn’t enough? Role of electrolytes explained by a dietitian</li>\n\n<h1>Why drinking water alone isn’t enough? Role of electrolytes explained by a dietitian</h1>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<p>“I’m drinking enough water, but I still feel low.” This is something many people experience, especially on busy days, during workouts, or in warmer weather. And often, the issue is not the quantity of water but what your body is actually receiving along with it. Hydration is not just about drinking fluids. It is about whether your system can properly absorb and circulate that fluid. For this, the body relies on electrolytes such as sodium, potassium, and magnesium, which quietly regulate fluid balance, muscle function, and steady energy levels. Without them, water passes through the body less than you expect.</p>\n\n<h2>Why can you feel “dehydrated” even after drinking water?</h2>\n\n<p>Think of days when you step out in the sun for a few hours. Or when you rush through work without eating on time. Or when your stomach has been upset. “Sometimes it is a hectic travel day. Sometimes it is mental stress that leaves you unusually drained,” Dietitian Lavleen Kaur tells Health Shots. In all these situations, the body is not just losing fluids. It is also losing key minerals. And that changes how you feel.</p>\n\n<p>You may keep sipping water throughout the day and still notice:</p>\n\n<li>A dull headache that refuses to go</li>\n\n<li>Mild dizziness when you stand up</li>\n\n<li>Muscles feel tight or heavy</li>\n\n<li>Energy is dipping faster than usual</li>\n\n<li>It feels like dehydration. But it is actually an imbalance.</li>\n\n<h2>What is your body really asking for?</h2>\n\n<p>When electrolyte levels drop, the body struggles to retain the water you drink. That is why plain water may not be enough in certain moments. The solution is not complicated. In fact, it is very familiar.</p>\n\n<li>A glass of coconut water.</li>\n\n<li>Nimbu paani with a small pinch of salt.</li>\n\n<li>A bowl of homemade chaas with lunch.</li>\n\n<li>Fruits like banana, mosambi, or orange throughout the day.</li>\n\n<p>These are not “health hacks”. They are traditional, everyday choices that quietly restore what the body has lost.</p>\n\n<h2>What happens if you drink too much water without electrolytes?</h2>\n\n<p>Drinking excessive amounts of water without replenishing minerals can further dilute the body’s natural electrolyte levels. This can leave you feeling more tired rather than refreshed. It is a case where doing more does not help. Doing it right does.</p>\n\n<h2>Does drinking water dehydrate you?</h2>\n\n<p>If you have been doing everything “correctly” and still not feeling your best, you are not alone. And you are not wrong. You may need to shift from “more water” to “better hydration”. That small understanding can change how you feel throughout the day, affecting your energy, focus, and overall sense of well-being.</p>\n\n<p></p>\n\n<p>Get latest updates on health and wellness along with Healthy Eating, Nutrition, Recipes, Superfoods</p>\n\n<p>Disclaimer: At Health Shots, we are committed to providing accurate, reliable, and authentic information to support your health and well-being. However, the content on this website is intended solely for informational purposes and should not be considered a substitute for professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalised advice regarding your specific medical condition or concerns. Read More</p>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<p>Tavishi Dogra is a health journalist with over 8 years of experience in the field. She has built a reputation as a trusted voice, adept at simplifying complex medical information for a broad audience. Her work with prominent media outlets, including RSTV, Financial Express, Jagran, and Zee, has honed her skills in effectively communicating health topics to diverse groups. Tavishi's extensive research and expertise in AYUSH (Ayurveda, Yoga, Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha, and Homoeopathy) make her a valuable source of expert advice and the latest updates on leading a healthier lifestyle. Follow her on HealthShots for more insights!</p>\n\n<p>Your response has been recorded to help us improve your experience on Health Shots</p>\n\n<li>I did not find the information I was looking for</li>\n\n<li>The Information was insufficient or incorrect</li>\n\n<li>I was looking for a medical advice</li>\n\n<li>I have another related question</li>\n\n<li>Others</li>\n\n<p>Didnt find what your were looking for? Ask HealthShot's AI powered chatbot!</p>\n\n<h2>Related Stories</h2>\n\n<h2>Photo Gallery</h2>\n\n<li>Emotional Health</li>\n\n<li>Happiness Hacks</li>\n\n<li>Mental Health</li>\n\n<li>Feminine Hygiene</li>\n\n<li>Menstruation</li>\n\n<li>Sexual Health</li>\n\n<li>Hair Care</li>\n\n<li>Natural Cures</li>\n\n<li>Skin Cares</li>\n\n<li>Muscle Gain</li>\n\n<li>Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Staying Fit</li>\n\n<li>Family Care</li>\n\n<li>Reproductive Care</li>\n\n<li>Self Care</li>\n\n<li>Nutrition</li>\n\n<li>Recipes</li>\n\n<li>SuperFoods</li>\n\n<li>Nutrimeter</li>\n\n<li>Quiz</li>\n\n<li>Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Home remedies</li>\n\n<li>Healthy Foods</li>\n\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n\n<li>Skin Care</li>\n\n<li>Hair Loss</li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li>7 nutrition tips every woman must know for a healthy life</li>\n\n<li>Snacking can harm your heart: 5 hidden dangers of ultra-processed foods</li>\n\n<li>Why do you have low vitamin D levels? Dietitian reveals 10 likely reasons</li>\n\n<li>5 high-protein foods worth prioritising while on GLP-1 medications</li>\n\n<li>Add apple cider vinegar to your diet if you’re trying to lose weight</li>\n\n<li>Best omega-3 fatty acid supplements: 9 top options for overall health</li>\n\n<li>6 multivitamins for women to increase immunity and energy</li>\n\n<li>Best magnesium supplements: 11 top picks transform your health</li>\n\n<li>Watermelon vs muskmelon: Which melon boosts your heart health more?</li>\n\n<li>How to get 50–100g of protein daily on a vegetarian diet without eating meat or eggs?</li>\n\n<li>We got India’s top diabetologist to share the 3 essentials for strong immunity</li>\n\n<li>Nutritionist suggests 10 anti-inflammatory foods that may help prevent </li>\n\n<li>Depression</li>\n\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n\n<li>Heart Failure</li>\n\n<li>Uterine Fibroids</li>\n\n<li>Mpox (Monkeypox)</li>\n\n<li>Vitiligo</li>\n\n<li>Covid-19</li>\n\n<li>Brain tumour</li>\n\n<li>Rosacea</li>\n\n<li>Kidney </li>\n\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)</li>\n\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n\n<li>Cholera</li>\n\n<li>Parkinson’s disease</li>\n\n<li>Chronic Obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li>\n\n<li>Cervical </li>\n\n<li>Lupus</li>\n\n<li>Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)</li>\n\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n\n<li>Breast </li>\n\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n\n<li>Autoimmune Disease</li>\n\n<li>Glaucoma</li>\n\n<li>Rabies</li>\n\n<li>Terms of Use</li>\n\n<li>Privacy Policy</li>\n\n<li>About Us</li>\n\n<li>Partner With Us</li>\n\n<li>RSS</li>\n\n<li>Sitemap</li>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":12,"title":"Ignoring the fourth trimester? All about postpartum care that every new mother needs to know","content":"<li></li>\n\n<li>Log Out</li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li>Log Out</li>\n\n<li>Switch to हिन्दी</li>\n\n<li>Healthy Eating Nutrition Recipes Superfoods</li>\n\n<li>Nutrition</li>\n\n<li>Recipes</li>\n\n<li>Superfoods</li>\n\n<li>Beauty Hair Care Natural Cures Skin Care</li>\n\n<li>Hair Care</li>\n\n<li>Natural Cures</li>\n\n<li>Skin Care</li>\n\n<li>Intimate Health Feminine Hygiene Menstruation Sexual Health</li>\n\n<li>Feminine Hygiene</li>\n\n<li>Menstruation</li>\n\n<li>Sexual Health</li>\n\n<li>Preventive Care Family Care Reproductive Care Self Care</li>\n\n<li>Family Care</li>\n\n<li>Reproductive Care</li>\n\n<li>Self Care</li>\n\n<li>How To</li>\n\n<li>Recommends</li>\n\n<li>Fitness Muscle Gain Staying Fit Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Muscle Gain</li>\n\n<li>Staying Fit</li>\n\n<li>Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Mind Emotional Health Happiness Hacks Mental Health</li>\n\n<li>Emotional Health</li>\n\n<li>Happiness Hacks</li>\n\n<li>Mental Health</li>\n\n<li>Brand Posts</li>\n\n<li>More Switch to हिन्दी Mind Daily Health Diseases Web Stories Health News She Slays Mom Says Horoscope Videos Podcasts Quizzes Quick Shots I Need Help Meet The Experts Rss Feed Partner With Us BMI Calculator About Us Personalise your feed Hindustan Times Live Hindustan Livemint</li>\n\n<li>Switch to हिन्दी</li>\n\n<li>Mind</li>\n\n<li>Daily Health</li>\n\n<li>Diseases</li>\n\n<li>Web Stories</li>\n\n<li>Health News</li>\n\n<li>She Slays</li>\n\n<li>Mom Says</li>\n\n<li>Horoscope</li>\n\n<li>Videos</li>\n\n<li>Podcasts</li>\n\n<li>Quizzes</li>\n\n<li>Quick Shots</li>\n\n<li>I Need Help</li>\n\n<li>Meet The Experts</li>\n\n<li>Rss Feed</li>\n\n<li>Partner With Us</li>\n\n<li>BMI Calculator</li>\n\n<li>About Us</li>\n\n<li>Personalise your feed</li>\n\n<li>Hindustan Times</li>\n\n<li>Live Hindustan</li>\n\n<li>Livemint</li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li>Mother's Day</li>\n\n<li>postpartum depression</li>\n\n<li>Reproductive Care</li>\n\n<li>Family Care</li>\n\n<li>Self Care</li>\n\n<li>Women’s Health</li>\n\n<li>Preventive Care</li>\n\n<li>Reproductive Care</li>\n\n<li>Ignoring the fourth trimester? All about postpartum care that every new mother needs to know</li>\n\n<h1>Ignoring the fourth trimester? All about postpartum care that every new mother needs to know</h1>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<p>The moment a baby is born, the spotlight shifts almost entirely to the newborn. The mother, who has just completed one of the most physically and emotionally demanding experiences of her life, quietly moves to the background. This is the fourth trimester, the 12 weeks after childbirth, and for millions of women, it remains the most underserved chapter of maternal healthcare. The physical recovery is visible, but the emotional weight a new mother carries is rarely spoken about, let alone addressed.</p>\n\n<h2>The invisible toll of new motherhood</h2>\n\n<p>Managing a newborn’s constant needs, recovering physically, navigating a new identity, and often returning to household responsibilities creates a compounding emotional load that builds silently. Postpartum depression affects approximately 22% of new mothers in India, yet it remains significantly underdiagnosed, as per the Journal of Neurosciences in Rural Practice.</p>\n\n<p>“Emotional exhaustion and social withdrawal often go unnoticed because the symptoms do not always appear as people expect.. Add to this the cultural tendency to frame all discomfort as normal, and many women spend months pushing through experiences that genuinely warrant attention,” Dr Neelam Suri, Senior Consultant Obstetrics &amp; Gynaecology, Indraprastha Apollo Hospital, Delhi, tells Health Shots.</p>\n\n<h2>Signs that should not be ignored</h2>\n\n<p>Postpartum recovery involves the whole body, and several signs point to a need for proper medical support:</p>\n\n<li>Persistent sadness, tearfulness, or feeling disconnected from the baby</li>\n\n<li>Extreme fatigue that does not improve with rest</li>\n\n<li>Pelvic pain, urinary leakage, or pressure in the lower abdomen</li>\n\n<li>Wound discomfort or delayed healing after a C-section</li>\n\n<li>Difficulty concentrating, anxiety, or intrusive thoughts</li>\n\n<h2>What should postpartum care include?</h2>\n\n<p>A well-structured postpartum plan goes well beyond a single six-week check-up. It should actively address:</p>\n\n<li>Lactation support: Breastfeeding difficulties are common and often go unsupported; early, consistent guidance improves outcomes for both mother and baby</li>\n\n<li>Postpartum exercise: Gradual, guided movements help restore core strength and pelvic floor function and should be introduced at an appropriate pace under proper supervision.</li>\n\n<li>Vaccination: Both the mother and the newborn have specific immunisation needs in the postpartum period that health professionals often overlook during routine follow-up.</li>\n\n<li>Contraception counselling: Family planning should be included in early postpartum consultations rather than left to chance or deferred to a later visit.</li>\n\n<li>Diet planning: A structured nutrition plan addressing post-pregnancy depletion of iron, calcium, and essential vitamins supports faster, more complete healing</li>\n\n<li>Returning to pre-pregnancy weight and shape: This is a real, valid concern for most new mothers. With the right guidance on safe timelines, progressive exercise, and balanced nutrition, it is an achievable goal without pressure or unrealistic expectations.</li>\n\n<h2>The IVF pregnancies that need more, not less</h2>\n\n<p>As IVF pregnancies steadily increase in urban India, the importance of postpartum follow-up grows even more. “These pregnancies often involve higher physical and emotional stakes, making structured, ongoing maternal care not just beneficial but essential. The fourth trimester in these cases demands a more personalised, closely monitored care plan,” says Dr Suri. The fourth trimester is not a footnote to pregnancy. It warrants the same clinical attention, structured planning, and consistent support as the nine months that preceded it.</p>\n\n<p></p>\n\n<p>Get latest updates on health and wellness along with Preventive Care, Family Care, Reproductive Care, Self Care</p>\n\n<p>Disclaimer: At Health Shots, we are committed to providing accurate, reliable, and authentic information to support your health and well-being. However, the content on this website is intended solely for informational purposes and should not be considered a substitute for professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalised advice regarding your specific medical condition or concerns. Read More</p>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<p>Tavishi Dogra is a health journalist with over 8 years of experience in the field. She has built a reputation as a trusted voice, adept at simplifying complex medical information for a broad audience. Her work with prominent media outlets, including RSTV, Financial Express, Jagran, and Zee, has honed her skills in effectively communicating health topics to diverse groups. Tavishi's extensive research and expertise in AYUSH (Ayurveda, Yoga, Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha, and Homoeopathy) make her a valuable source of expert advice and the latest updates on leading a healthier lifestyle. Follow her on HealthShots for more insights!</p>\n\n<h2>Related Stories</h2>\n\n<h2>Photo Gallery</h2>\n\n<li>Emotional Health</li>\n\n<li>Happiness Hacks</li>\n\n<li>Mental Health</li>\n\n<li>Feminine Hygiene</li>\n\n<li>Menstruation</li>\n\n<li>Sexual Health</li>\n\n<li>Hair Care</li>\n\n<li>Natural Cures</li>\n\n<li>Skin Cares</li>\n\n<li>Muscle Gain</li>\n\n<li>Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Staying Fit</li>\n\n<li>Family Care</li>\n\n<li>Reproductive Care</li>\n\n<li>Self Care</li>\n\n<li>Nutrition</li>\n\n<li>Recipes</li>\n\n<li>SuperFoods</li>\n\n<li>Nutrimeter</li>\n\n<li>Quiz</li>\n\n<li>Weight Loss</li>\n\n<li>Home remedies</li>\n\n<li>Healthy Foods</li>\n\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n\n<li>Skin Care</li>\n\n<li>Hair Loss</li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li></li>\n\n<li>Why do your sugar levels fluctuate more in summer?</li>\n\n<li>Is high blood pressure a hidden sign of a thyroid problem?</li>\n\n<li>Can heatwave make PMOS symptoms worse?</li>\n\n<li>Is your tiredness more than burnout? Know all about chronic fatigue syndrome</li>\n\n<li>How to stay safe from Hantavirus if you’re travelling abroad?</li>\n\n<li>Why are heart attacks becoming more common in 20s, 30s and 40s?</li>\n\n<li>Working long hours? New study shows how it increases obesity risk</li>\n\n<li>Are you ignoring these 4 silent signs of ovarian ?</li>\n\n<li>Norovirus vs Hantavirus: Are these two viruses spreading on cruise ships the same?</li>\n\n<li>Do all breast  patients need chemotherapy?</li>\n\n<li>Ignoring the fourth trimester? All about postpartum care that every new mother needs to know</li>\n\n<li>5 everyday habits that may be silently raising your cholesterol levels</li>\n\n<li>Depression</li>\n\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n\n<li>Heart Failure</li>\n\n<li>Uterine Fibroids</li>\n\n<li>Mpox (Monkeypox)</li>\n\n<li>Vitiligo</li>\n\n<li>Covid-19</li>\n\n<li>Brain tumour</li>\n\n<li>Rosacea</li>\n\n<li>Kidney </li>\n\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)</li>\n\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n\n<li>Cholera</li>\n\n<li>Parkinson’s disease</li>\n\n<li>Chronic Obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li>\n\n<li>Cervical </li>\n\n<li>Lupus</li>\n\n<li>Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)</li>\n\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n\n<li>Breast </li>\n\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n\n<li>Autoimmune Disease</li>\n\n<li>Glaucoma</li>\n\n<li>Rabies</li>\n\n<li>Terms of Use</li>\n\n<li>Privacy Policy</li>\n\n<li>About Us</li>\n\n<li>Partner With Us</li>\n\n<li>RSS</li>\n\n<li>Sitemap</li>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":13,"title":"Understanding and Preventing Hepatitis B in Pregnancy: A Global Perspective","content":"<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Hepatitis B is a viral infection that affects the liver, causing potential long-term complications such as chronic liver damage, cirrhosis, or liver cancer if left untreated. The risk of transmission from mother to child during childbirth is particularly concerning, with babies infected at birth facing a significantly higher risk of developing chronic hepatitis B. It is essential to understand how transmission occurs and the steps that can be taken to prevent it, ensuring the health and well-being of both mother and child.</span></p>\n\n<h2><b>What is Hepatitis B and How is it Transmitted?</b></h2>\n\n<p>Hepatitis B spreads through contact with infected blood or bodily fluids, commonly through unprotected sexual contact, sharing needles or syringes, and mother-to-child transmission during birth. Many individuals infected with hepatitis B, especially infants and young children, may not exhibit any symptoms, emphasizing the importance of screening.</p>\n\n<p>When symptoms do appear, they may include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent fatigue or tiredness</li>\n<li>Yellowing of the skin and the whites of the eyes (jaundice)</li>\n<li>Dark-colored urine</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain, especially in the upper right area</li>\n<li>Loss of appetite</li>\n<li>Nausea and vomiting</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If any of these symptoms occur, it is crucial to consult a healthcare professional for a proper evaluation.</p>\n\n<h2><b>Mother-to-Child Transmission of Hepatitis B</b></h2>\n\n<p>During childbirth, a baby can come into contact with the mother's blood and vaginal fluids, allowing the virus to enter the baby's bloodstream if the mother is infected and no preventive measures are taken. Newborns are more susceptible to developing a chronic, lifelong infection due to their immature immune systems.</p>\n\n<p>It is essential to note that hepatitis B is not typically transmitted through casual contact, such as hugging, sharing utensils, or breastfeeding, provided the infant receives proper preventive care. Knowing one's status early in pregnancy enables mothers and healthcare providers to plan the best course of action.</p>\n\n<h2><b>Preventing Mother-to-Child Transmission of Hepatitis B</b></h2>\n\n<p>Several measures can significantly reduce the risk of passing hepatitis B to a baby. These should be discussed with a healthcare provider as early as possible, ideally during the first prenatal visit.</p>\n\n<h3><b>1. Early Screening and Diagnosis</b></h3>\n<p>All pregnant individuals should be tested for hepatitis B early in pregnancy. If the test is positive, a healthcare provider can monitor the viral load and liver function throughout the pregnancy, ensuring appropriate care and minimizing risks.</p>\n\n<h3><b>2. Vaccination and Immunization</b></h3>\n<p>The hepatitis B vaccine is a highly effective way to protect newborns. The first dose should be administered within 24 hours of birth, followed by two or three additional doses over the next few months. A healthcare provider will provide a schedule for the vaccinations.</p>\n\n<h3><b>3. Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin (HBIG) Administration</b></h3>\n<p>Babies born to mothers with hepatitis B should receive a shot of hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG) within 12 hours of birth. HBIG provides immediate, short-term protection while the vaccine takes effect, reducing the risk of transmission.</p>\n\n<h3><b>4. Antiviral Medication for Pregnant Individuals</b></h3>\n<p>For pregnant individuals with a high viral load, antiviral medication taken during the third trimester can lower the amount of virus in the blood and further reduce the risk of passing it to the baby. This treatment is temporary and closely monitored by a healthcare provider.</p>\n\n<h3><b>5. Safe Delivery and Breastfeeding Practices</b></h3>\n<p>Vaginal delivery is generally safe for individuals with hepatitis B. Breastfeeding is also safe, provided the baby has received the hepatitis B vaccine and HBIG at birth. Regular check-ups with a healthcare provider are essential to monitor liver health and vaccination status.</p>\n\n<h2><b>Empowering Individuals with Knowledge</b></h2>\n<p>Hepatitis B is a manageable condition. With the right care and knowledge, individuals can significantly reduce the chances of passing it to their babies. Early testing, proper vaccination, and medical follow-up are crucial tools in preventing transmission. Every individual deserves access to accurate information and supportive healthcare, enabling them to take proactive steps to safeguard their family's health.</p><p><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"/articles/article-images/1779414090_tungart7-ai-generated-8881541_640.jpg","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":14,"title":"Why Am I Always Hungry? Understanding the Causes and Finding Solutions","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Hunger is a natural signal from the body indicating the need for nutrients and energy. However, constant hunger can be a frustrating issue for many women, and there may be other reasons at play. Persistent hunger in women may be due to several different factors, including hormonal imbalances, stress, anxiety, and depression.</span></p>\n\n<h3>Reasons for Constant Hunger</h3>\n\n<p>Understanding the factors that contribute to constant hunger is crucial for overcoming this issue and maintaining a healthy relationship with food. Some of the possible causes of constant hunger include blood sugar imbalances, lack of protein, dietary fibre deficiency, hormonal fluctuations, lifestyle factors, and emotional eating.</p>\n\n<h3>Blood Sugar Imbalances</h3>\n\n<p>A recent study shows that people who experience big dips in blood sugar during the day feel hungrier and eat more. Diets high in quickly digested carbohydrates, low in fibre, and high in sugar can cause more pronounced spikes and drops in blood sugar levels, leading to increased hunger soon after eating.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Eat complex carbohydrates such as whole grains, legumes, and vegetables, which digest slowly and provide steady energy.</li>\n  <li>Combine complex carbohydrates with lean proteins like chicken, fish, tofu, or beans to further stabilise blood sugar and enhance satiety.</li>\n  <li>Eat a balanced meal every 3-4 hours to avoid significant drops that can lead to overeating.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Lack of Protein</h3>\n\n<p>Protein is vital for satiety as it helps regulate the hormones that control hunger. Peptide YY is a hormone released by the gut in response to eating, and it helps to reduce appetite.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Include some protein with every meal, such as chickpeas, quinoa, lentils, tofu, organic lean meat and fish, eggs, nuts.</li>\n  <li>Add a scoop of protein shake to your morning smoothie.</li>\n  <li>Snack on protein-rich foods, such as nuts and seeds, slices of apples with nut butter, small tin of tuna, or a boiled egg, to keep hunger at bay.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Dietary Fibre Deficiency</h3>\n\n<p>Fibre slows down digestion and absorption, helping you stay fuller for longer and reducing the likelihood of overeating. Adults are advised to consume 30g of fibre a day, but current figures suggest we are only getting around 60% (18g) of this.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Include more fibrous foods in your diet, such as vegetables, fruit, nuts, seeds, pulses, beans, wholegrains.</li>\n  <li>Try a fibre supplement, such as Viridian’s Organic Prebio Fibre Powder.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Hormonal Fluctuations</h3>\n\n<p>Hormonal fluctuations can significantly influence women’s hunger levels. For example, during different life stages such as menstruation, pregnancy, and menopause, hormones like oestrogen and progesterone fluctuate, impacting appetite.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Track your menstrual cycle to anticipate periods of heightened cravings.</li>\n  <li>Focus on balanced meals with complex carbohydrates, lean proteins, and healthy fats to stabilise blood sugar levels and improve satiety between meals.</li>\n  <li>Plan and prep healthy meals and snacks in advance, especially before the luteal phase.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Lifestyle Factors</h3>\n\n<p>A lack of sleep can disrupt the balance of hunger hormones. Stress also plays a role in hunger levels. When stressed, the body releases cortisol, a hormone that can increase appetite and lead to emotional eating.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Aim for 7-9 hours of quality sleep per night to regulate ghrelin, the hunger hormone, and leptin, the satiety hormone, to reduce cravings.</li>\n  <li>Try incorporating mindfulness meditation, yoga, or deep breathing exercises into your daily routine to manage stress.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Emotional Eating</h3>\n\n<p>Emotional well-being significantly impacts hunger and eating patterns. Stress, low mood, and hormonal changes can also impact your appetite and trigger those hunger cravings which may cause you to reach for unhealthy foods.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Find ways to manage your emotions better, such as identifying triggers, keeping a food diary, and implementing stress management techniques like mindfulness meditation or deep breathing exercises.</li>\n  <li>Establish healthier coping mechanisms, such as engaging in activities that you enjoy and that distract you from emotional triggers.</li>\n  <li>Consider seeking professional help, such as counselling or cognitive behavioural therapy.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>By understanding the causes of constant hunger and implementing these solutions, women can develop a healthier relationship with food and improve their overall well-being.<br><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
{"id":15,"title":"Unlocking the Secrets of Longevity: A Journey to a Blue Zone Paradise","content":"<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \" segoe=\"\" ui\",=\"\" roboto,=\"\" sans-serif;\"=\"\">Located in the heart of the Mediterranean, a picturesque island boasts stunning beaches, vibrant culture, and a unique way of life that has captured the attention of wellness enthusiasts worldwide. This enchanting destination is one of the world's five Blue Zones, where people live significantly longer and healthier lives.</span></p>\n<p>The island's breathtaking landscapes, rich cultural heritage, and emphasis on wellness provide an idyllic escape for those seeking to rejuvenate their mind, body, and soul. With its luxurious resorts, thalassotherapy spas, and exquisite Mediterranean cuisine, this island paradise offers a unique blend of relaxation, culture, and wellness.</p>\n\n<h2>The Blue Zone Phenomenon</h2>\n<p>This extraordinary region is renowned for its high number of centenarians, and researchers have identified several factors contributing to this remarkable longevity. These include a healthy diet, active lifestyle, strong family ties, and a supportive community. The Mediterranean diet, in particular, plays a pivotal role, emphasizing fresh, local ingredients and balanced nutrition.</p>\n<p>A healthy diet is rich in nutrients, including fruits, vegetables, legumes, nuts, and seeds, providing essential vitamins, minerals, and fiber. The diet also includes healthy fats, such as those found in olive oil, which help reduce bad cholesterol levels and lower the risk of heart disease.</p>\n\n<h3>Key Components of a Healthy Diet</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Rich in nutrients, including fruits, vegetables, legumes, nuts, and seeds</li>\n<li>Includes healthy fats, such as those found in olive oil</li>\n<li>Emphasizes whole grains, lean proteins, and moderate consumption of wine</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>A Gateway to Wellness</h2>\n<p>Luxurious resorts on the island offer a unique blend of comfort, culture, and wellness. With their commitment to eco-sustainability and authentic hospitality, these resorts provide the perfect setting for immersing oneself in the island's Blue Zone lifestyle. From relaxing in luxurious rooms to indulging in world-class thalassotherapy spas, every aspect of the resort is designed to promote wellness and rejuvenation.</p>\n<p>The resorts' thalassotherapy spas harness the healing properties of the sea, offering treatments designed to detoxify, rejuvenate, and revitalize the body. Guests can indulge in a variety of treatments, including seawater hydrotherapy, algae wraps, and marine mud baths, which are known to improve circulation, reduce stress, and alleviate various ailments.</p>\n\n<h3>Thalassotherapy Treatments</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Seawater hydrotherapy</li>\n<li>Algae wraps</li>\n<li>Marine mud baths</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The Mediterranean Diet: A Blueprint for Health</h2>\n<p>The Mediterranean diet is celebrated worldwide for its health benefits and is a cornerstone of the island's way of life. This diet emphasizes fresh, seasonal produce, whole grains, lean proteins, and healthy fats, primarily from olive oil. Moderate consumption of wine, particularly red wine, is also part of the diet, due to its high antioxidant content.</p>\n<p>The Mediterranean diet is not only healthy but also sustainable, focusing on locally sourced, seasonal ingredients. This approach supports local agriculture and reduces the environmental impact associated with food production.</p>\n\n<h3>Benefits of the Mediterranean Diet</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Rich in nutrients</li>\n<li>Includes healthy fats</li>\n<li>Emphasizes whole grains, lean proteins, and moderate consumption of wine</li>\n<li>Sustainable and environmentally friendly</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>A Taste of the Island: Local Wineries</h2>\n<p>No visit to the island is complete without experiencing its exquisite wines. Local wineries are a testament to the island's rich winemaking tradition, producing some of the finest wines in the region. From exceptional Vermentino and Carignano varieties to other unique blends, the island's wines are a must-try for any wine enthusiast.</p>\n<p>Visitors can immerse themselves in the island's winemaking culture by visiting local wineries, where they can learn about the production process, taste some of the finest wines, and take home a bottle or two to share with friends and family.</p>\n\n<h2>Embracing the Island Way of Life</h2>\n<p>The island's status as a Blue Zone offers valuable insights into the secrets of longevity and wellness. By embracing the principles of the Mediterranean diet, indulging in the therapeutic benefits of thalassotherapy, and savouring the island's exceptional wines, visitors can experience a taste of the island way of life.</p>\n<p>Whether seeking relaxation, adventure, or a deeper connection with nature, the island and its luxurious resorts offer a unique blend of luxury and well-being, providing the perfect setting for a rejuvenating and unforgettable experience.</p><p><br></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 13px; line-height: normal; font-family: \" helvetica=\"\" neue\";=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(0,=\"\" 0,=\"\" 0);\"=\"\"><i>Disclaimer: This article is intended for informational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider for personalized guidance regarding your pregnancy and health concerns.</i></p>","category":"","image_url":"","created_at":"2026-06-20 15:14:05","img":"","link":"","source":"","freeArticle":false}
